## Final H3 transient run – 3 October 2026

A strut-resolved transient CFD implementation test was completed using the
released-stent lumen geometry.

### Run configuration

- Mesh: 1,449,529 cells
- Cardiac period: 0.8 s
- Time step: 0.04 s
- Number of time steps: 20
- Maximum iterations per time step: 20
- Inlet: pulsatile `inlet_velocity` UDF
- Outlet: 0 Pa gauge pressure
- Artery wall: no-slip
- Strut wall: no-slip
- TAWSS / OSI: `osi.c` UDF
- Solver: transient pressure-based, Coupled pressure–velocity scheme

### Recorded results

| Quantity | Result |
|---|---:|
| Inlet mass flow | 2.35905437E-04 kg/s |
| Outlet mass flow | -2.35891215E-04 kg/s |
| Relative mass imbalance | 0.00603% |
| Inlet static pressure | 223.93649 Pa |
| Outlet static pressure | 0 Pa |
| Pressure drop | 223.93649 Pa |
| Maximum velocity at t = 0.8 s | 0.2171524 m/s |
| Instantaneous WSS average | 1.304153 Pa |
| Instantaneous WSS minimum | 0.000605337 Pa |
| Instantaneous WSS maximum | 24.27308 Pa |
| TAWSS average | 2.755128 Pa |
| TAWSS minimum | 0.01310555 Pa |
| TAWSS maximum | 80.7746 Pa |
| OSI average | 8.132387E-05 |
| OSI minimum | 3.107374E-09 |
| OSI maximum | 0.4807748 |

### Interpretation

The final-step mass imbalance was approximately 0.006%, indicating strong
mass conservation for the completed transient solution.

The wall-averaged OSI was very small, indicating predominantly unidirectional
wall shear over most of the artery. Local OSI reached approximately 0.481,
showing that highly oscillatory or reversing shear occurred in small localised
regions around the stented/stenotic section.

TAWSS and OSI contours, instantaneous WSS, velocity, pressure, mass-flow and
convergence evidence are stored under `evidence/`.

### Important limitation

This H3 run was performed as an implementation and integration test over one
0.8 s cardiac cycle.

It demonstrates that the pulsatile inlet, strut-resolved CFD model and
TAWSS/OSI workflow can operate together successfully. It does not by itself
establish cycle-to-cycle periodicity or time-step independence.

The final campaign implementation uses multiple cardiac cycles and evaluates
the final cycle after the transient solution has developed.